course: dictionary
lesson: 13236
title: Generate: making things happen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Generate is a verb. It means to produce or make something, like electricity, heat or power. «The wind turbines on the hill generate electricity for the whole village.» The turbines make electricity, so generate means make or produce.
explain: Generate can also mean to cause something or bring it about. «Our class discussion helped us generate lots of new ideas.» Nobody made the ideas with their hands. The talk caused them to appear. Ask yourself: is something being made, or caused to happen? Either way, generate fits.
explain: In maths, a rule can generate a list of numbers. «Start at 3 and add 4» generates 3, 7, 11, 15. The rule makes the sequence, one number after another. Other forms of the word are generates, generated and generating.
fact: Generate is a verb, so it tells you what someone or something does.
fact: To generate means to produce or make something, such as electricity, heat or power.
fact: To generate can also mean to cause something or bring it about, as in generate interest.
fact: «Randomly generated» means made by chance, without a person choosing each part.
fact: Forms of generate: generates (now), generated (before now), generating (happening now).
exercise: «The solar panels on the school roof generate electricity.» What does generate mean here? | *Make or produce | Lose | Break down | Wash
exercise: «The rule starts at 5 and adds 2, so it generates 5, 7, 9, 11.» What does the rule do? | *It makes the list of numbers | It removes every odd number | It counts backwards from 11 | It adds the numbers together
exercise: «The computer can generate a password in seconds.» Find and copy the word that means make. | computer | password | seconds | *generate
test: «Their plan generated a lot of interest in the school.» What does generated mean here? | Ended | Hid | *Caused or brought about | Counted
test: Yesterday the machine ___ a new password. Which word fills the gap? | generate | *generated | generates | generating
exam: Which sentence uses generate to mean cause something? | «They walked to the park together.» | «The rain fell all afternoon.» | «He sat down to eat his lunch.» | *«Her funny story generated laughter all round the table.»
